menu "ESP32 Watch"

    config WATCH_WIFI_SSID
        string "WiFi SSID"
        default ""
        help
            Leave this empty and the radio never comes up: the watch runs off
            its own boot clock and the WiFi corner readout stays dim, which is
            what that dimming is for. Fill it in and the watch joins the
            network and asks SNTP what time it is.

    config WATCH_WIFI_PASSWORD
        string "WiFi password"
        default ""

    config WATCH_SNTP_SERVER
        string "SNTP server"
        default "pool.ntp.org"

    config WATCH_TIMEZONE
        string "POSIX TZ string"
        default "CET-1CEST,M3.5.0,M10.5.0/3"
        help
            SNTP hands out UTC and the device has no locale to convert it
            with, so the zone is compiled in. The default is central Europe
            with EU daylight saving.

    config WATCH_VOICE_URL
        string "Speech server URL"
        default ""
        help
            Where the oMLX server that hears, thinks and speaks is, as a whole
            address: https://omlx.ai-at-home.de, or http://192.168.1.20:8000
            for one on the network the watch joins. Not 127.0.0.1, which on
            the watch means the watch.

            https makes the request go through TLS, checked against the
            certificate bundle ESP-IDF compiles in. http skips all of that and
            is what a server on the same network is reached at. The port is
            the scheme's own unless one is written down.

            Leave it empty and the assistant never starts: the watch is a
            clock, and the microphone and speaker corners stay dim, which is
            what that dimming is for.

            It needs an SSID above as well. All three services are on the
            other end of the radio, so no network means no assistant.

    config WATCH_VOICE_KEY
        string "Speech server API key"
        default ""
        help
            Sent as "Authorization: Bearer <key>" on every request. A server
            published on the internet checks one — the proxy in front of
            omlx.ai-at-home.de holds no secret of its own, so the service
            behind it is what does the checking.

            Leave it empty and no header is sent at all, which is what an oMLX
            on the same desk with its key verification off wants. An empty
            header is a rejected request rather than an unauthenticated one,
            so the two are not the same thing.

            It is compiled into the image. Anyone with the flash has it.

    config WATCH_STT_MODEL
        string "Transcribing model"
        default ""
        help
            Empty means the default in voice/turn.h, which is
            parakeet-tdt-0.6b-v3 and is where that name is written down. Fill
            it in only to name a different one — a model host serves several
            and swaps them, which is why this is configuration now.

    config WATCH_BRAIN_MODEL
        string "Chat model"
        default ""
        help
            What the assistant thinks with. Empty means the default in
            voice/turn.h, which is Qwen3.8-27B-oQ4e-mtp — the model this
            watch was built against. Fill it in to name a different one the
            server hosts.

            Write "echo" and there is no chat request at all: the answer is
            the question said back, word for word. It needs no third service
            and is the shortest path through the whole pipeline — a turn that
            breaks there breaks everywhere — so it is the mode to fall back to
            when the microphone or the speaker is what is being worked on.
            Thinking costs several seconds a turn on top of transcribing and
            speaking.

    config WATCH_TTS_MODEL
        string "Speaking model"
        default ""
        help
            Empty means the default in voice/turn.h, which is
            chatterbox-multilingual-v3. It ships no voices of its own, so the
            reference clip is what it borrows one from either way.

    config WATCH_LANGUAGE
        string "Language spoken"
        default ""
        help
            The language the synthesiser is told to speak, as a two-letter
            code. Empty means the default in voice/turn.h, which is "de". The
            transcriber detects its own and the chat model is asked to answer
            in whichever language it was spoken to, so this is the synthesiser
            alone.

    config WATCH_WAKE_PHRASE
        string "Wake phrase"
        default ""
        help
            What the watch answers to. There is no wake-word model on this
            board: everything the room says is transcribed, and the watch
            wakes when this turns up in the text.

            Leave it empty and it listens for "hey kai" and the five other
            spellings the transcriber has been seen to return for that name,
            which is the list in voice/turn.c and the only place it is
            written down.

            Fill it in and it listens for that instead. Case and punctuation
            do not matter. Give the spellings a transcriber is likely to
            produce, separated by '|' — a name it has never been shown is
            rarely written the same way twice:

                hey ada|hey adah|hi ada

            Match the greeting and not the name on its own, or every word
            that merely starts the same way wakes the watch.

endmenu
